package agentsdoc

import "strings"

// writeV04dKeysSection documents what 0.4d changes in the keys for an
// authoring agent: the new tokens, the decoder that reads the legacy, Kitty,
// and modifyOtherKeys forms, the keys an `input` takes, what `<hints>` shows
// for a terminal that cannot send a key, and `play --keyboard`. It names no
// path of the repository, since the project briefing (ProjectMarkdown) is this
// same text.
func writeV04dKeysSection(b *strings.Builder) {
	b.WriteString("### v0.4d: the keys\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- A `<tui>` without `version` also accepts the key tokens `delete`, `f1` to `f12`, `alt+` a letter (`a`-`z` or `A`-`Z`: `alt+A` is shift with alt) or a digit, `alt+enter`, `alt+backspace`, `shift+enter`, and `ctrl+enter`. Not tokens: `alt+` a punctuation character, `alt+space`, `shift+` a letter (write the upper-case letter), `ctrl+` a named key, `alt+` a named key other than `enter` and `backspace`, and anything after `f12`. An unknown token is `V003` with the list of tokens. In a document that writes `version` each new token is `V003`, `key \"delete\" (requires a <tui> without version)`, and an unknown key keeps the old list. `ctrl+i`, `ctrl+j`, and `ctrl+m` stay `V003` in a keymap of the current language.\n")
	b.WriteString("- `Run()` asks the terminal for the Kitty keyboard protocol (`CSI ? u` in the capability probe, outside the skip rule) and pushes flag 1 when it answers, so `esc` arrives at once as `CSI 27 u` and `ctrl+enter` and `shift+enter` are told apart from `enter`; without an answer it sets `modifyOtherKeys` level 2; with no probe (the output is not a terminal, or `TERM` is `dumb`) the session is legacy. The leave sequence undoes it on every path. `TUIMARK_LOG`'s `caps` record has `keyboard` (`kitty`, `modify-other-keys`, or `legacy`) for such a document only. A document that writes `version` gets none of this.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The decoder of the current language reads every form in every session: the legacy bytes, Kitty's `CSI CODE ; MODS u`, and `CSI 27 ; MODS ; CODE ~`, whatever the probe found. `ESC` followed by a letter or a digit, in one read or within the esc timeout, is `alt+` that character (`ESC x` is `alt+x`, not `esc` then `x`), `ESC CR` and `ESC LF` are `alt+enter`, and `ESC DEL` is `alt+backspace`. Modifiers that make no token are dropped, shift first, then alt, then ctrl (`CSI 1 ; 5 A` is `up`, `ctrl+backspace` is `backspace`), but `ctrl` with `space` or a printable character that is not a letter gives no key (`ctrl+space`, `ctrl+/`, `ctrl+3`), so a chord pressed for something else types nothing. A modified `enter` is no longer `enter` (`shift+enter` and `ctrl+enter` under Kitty): write `keys=\"enter,shift+enter\"` for a row that should fire for both. The Kitty keypad codes decode as the keys the keypad sent before.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The LF byte (`ctrl+j`) is a line break in a focused `textarea` and `enter` everywhere else; it is not a token. `left` and `right` held with `alt` or `ctrl` are still `left` and `right` for the keymap, and move a focused `input` by a word.\n")
	b.WriteString("- A focused `input` takes `delete`, `alt+b`, `alt+f`, and `alt+backspace` (a word is a run of characters that are not spaces), and never `ctrl+w` or `ctrl+k`, so a row bound to either still fires while it has focus.\n")
	b.WriteString("- A legacy terminal (and a `modifyOtherKeys` one, which cannot be confirmed) cannot send `shift+enter`, `ctrl+enter`, `alt+O`, `alt+P`, or `alt+X`, so a row that needs one also names a key every terminal sends (`keys=\"shift+enter,alt+enter\"`). `<hints>` shows the first key of a row that the session can send, and `scope=\"active\"` hides a row none of whose keys it can send; a `keycap` that is not empty is shown whatever the session. `dump`, `validate`, and `inspect` render as a Kitty session.\n")
	b.WriteString("- `tuimark play FILE --keyboard kitty|legacy` (`Keyboard: \"legacy\"` in Go, `\"keyboard\"` in a golden manifest entry with `input` or `script`, and `tuimark_play`'s `keyboard`) chooses the terminal `play` emulates: a key step is delivered as the bytes that terminal sends for the token (`CSI 13 ; 2 u` for `shift+enter` under Kitty, `ESC x` for `alt+x` under legacy) and decoded as `Run()` decodes them. A key a legacy terminal cannot send is a usage error. A document that writes `version` ignores `--keyboard`, and a key step naming a new token against it is a usage error with the `(requires a <tui> without version)` message.\n")
	b.WriteString("- Under `tuimark run`, `F12` (every key the decoder reads as `f12`, `shift+F12` included) toggles the event strip and never reaches the dispatch, so a row with `f12` fires under `Run()` and `host` but not under `run`; every other F key reaches the dispatch.\n\n")
}
